jemalloc set for a desktop shell rather than a server.

Its defaults: four arenas per CPU core, a thread's freed memory only handed
back to the system when a thread of the same arena allocates again (most of a
shell's ~140 threads rarely do), and blocks up to 32 KiB kept by each thread.
With 4 arenas, a background thread that returns freed memory and 4 KiB thread
caches, the shell's own memory is about 45 MB smaller right after start. In
the executable (jemalloc's `malloc_conf` symbol) rather than MALLOC_CONF in
the launcher: the programs the shell starts would inherit the variable.

--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -1,3 +1,16 @@
 #include "launch/main.hpp"
 
+// jemalloc's settings, read when it starts (the MALLOC_CONF environment
+// variable, read after, still overrides them). Its defaults suit a server:
+// four arenas per CPU core, memory a thread freed only handed back to the
+// system when a thread of the same arena allocates again, and up to 32 KiB
+// blocks kept by each thread. A shell has one busy thread and over a hundred
+// that mostly sleep: 4 arenas, a background thread that returns freed memory,
+// and only blocks up to 4 KiB kept per thread.
+extern "C" {
+// NOLINTNEXTLINE
+const char* malloc_conf =
+    "background_thread:true,max_background_threads:1,narenas:4,tcache_max:4096";
+}
+
 int main(int argc, char** argv) { return qs::launch::main(argc, argv); }
